I'll create a function that finds duplicates in an array with O(n) time and space complexity, preserving the order of first discovery.

```js
function findDuplicates(arr) {
    const seen = new Set();
    const duplicates = new Set();

    for (const item of arr) {
        if (seen.has(item)) {
            duplicates.add(item);
        } else {
            seen.add(item);
        }
    }

    return Array.from(duplicates);
}
```

**Complexity Analysis:**
- **Time Complexity:** O(n) - We iterate through the array once (n operations), and each Set operation (has/add) is O(1) on average.
- **Space Complexity:** O(n) - In the worst case, we store all unique elements in the `seen` Set and all duplicates in the `duplicates` Set.

**JavaScript Set Iteration Order:**
JavaScript Sets maintain insertion order as of ES6. When we convert the `duplicates` Set to an array with `Array.from(duplicates)`, the elements appear in the order they were added to the Set. In our implementation, this corresponds to the order each duplicate was first discovered in the input array.

**Boundary Cases:**
- Empty array: Returns an empty array
- Negative numbers: Handled correctly (e.g., -1 and 1 are different values)
- Strings: Handled correctly (e.g., "1" and 1 are different values)
- Mixed types: Handled correctly (e.g., 1 and "1" are different values)
- Repeated values: Only added to duplicates Set on the second occurrence
- Single element: Returns empty array (no duplicates)

**Example Usage:**
```js
console.log(findDuplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 5, 5, 5])); // [2, 1, 5]
console.log(findDuplicates([])); // []
console.log(findDuplicates([1, "1", 1, "1"])); // [1, "1"]
console.log(findDuplicates([-1, 0, -1, 2, -1])); // [-1]
```

The function efficiently tracks seen values and duplicates using Sets, ensuring optimal performance while maintaining the required order of first discovery.

